--TEST--
`foreach (… as inout $v)` is refused over a subject with no storage to write back to, and over a binding wider than the element type — `rule:iteration/foreach-subjects`, `rule:types/declaration`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `inout $v` writes each element back where it came from, so there has to be
// somewhere to leave it: a temporary has none, and `rule:iteration/two-interfaces` gives a
// cursor `advance()`/`current()` and no element storage at all. PHP refuses
// both, the cursor by name. And a by-reference binding *writes* as well as
// reads, so the covariance an ordinary `array<T>` read has — an `array<int>`
// read as `mixed` — would store the wider type into the array.
final class Seq implements Iterator<int> {
    private int $n = 0;
    public function advance(): bool {
        $this->n = $this->n + 1;
        return $this->n < 3;
    }
    public function current(): int {
        return $this->n;
    }
}

final class Rows {
    public static function of(): array<int> {
        return [1, 2];
    }
}

foreach (Rows::of() as inout int $fresh) {
    $fresh = 0;
}
foreach (new Seq() as inout int $step) {
    $step = 0;
}
array<int> $counts = [1, 2];
foreach ($counts as inout mixed $wide) {
    $wide = 0;
}
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0490]: only a variable can be iterated by reference
   --> case.nvs:25:10
%A
error[E0490]: an `Iterable`/`Iterator` subject has no element to bind by reference
   --> case.nvs:28:23
%A
error[E0491]: a by-reference binding over an `array<int>` needs the type `int` exactly, not `mixed`
   --> case.nvs:32:21
%A
error: aborting due to 3 errors
